// ff8_accessibility.h - Core header for FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod
//
// Lean post-v0.15.12.0 cleanup. The inline-changelog chain that had
// accreted on the macro line (421 KB by v0.15.11.0) was moved to
// `ff8_accessibility_history.h`, which is NOT included by the build.
// Going forward this header holds only the version macro and the
// system includes other modules rely on it to transitively provide.
//
// The CANONICAL changelog lives in `CHANGELOG.md` at the project root.
// Older entries (pre-v0.15.12.0) are preserved in `CHANGELOG_HISTORY.md`.
//
// FF8 runtime address resolution: see `ff8_addresses.h` /
// `ff8_addresses.cpp` for the resolver that computes addresses at
// runtime using the same offset-chain technique as FFNx.

#pragma once

#include <windows.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>

// ================================================================
// FF8 Original PC Accessibility Mod version
// Increment on every build change. Must match the top `## vX.Y.Z`
// heading in CHANGELOG.md or `Utilities/push_to_github.ps1` will
// refuse to push.
// ================================================================
#define FF8OPC_VERSION "0.18.3.87"  // v0.18.3.87: #67 -- YAW-BASED SCREEN-RELATIVE 8-WAY on-foot steering (the executor fix the road work isolated). The .86 road-preference BAT SOLVED ROUTING: the [PLAN]/[ROUTEMAP] route follows road cells up col 99 then bends east along the road to Dollet, and F11 showed Squall PHYSICALLY STANDING ON THE PAVED ROAD -- but he still wedged at the START (idx=0/32, ~19.5km from this farther save), oscillating, and the trace shows EXACTLY why and it is NOT routing: he is ON the road, the road runs due north here (tgtBrg ~4070 ~= 358deg), but hdg=3752 is FROZEN (the fixed per-region CAMERA YAW ~= 330deg), so the .80 heading turn-then-go pressed pure UP -- which on foot WALKS at the yaw (NNW), ~28deg off the road -- and that drift carried him off the thin road ribbon into the cliff beside it (d+0 on contact; reverse shoved him back; UP walked him into it again). The heading-law can only press pure cardinals (UP, or R/L pivot), so it can never aim the DIAGONAL needed to track a road bearing between N and NE. This is the .80 unify failure: .80 routed on-foot onto the vehicle heading law on the (correct) finding that UP walks at the yaw, but a yaw you can READ is not a facing you can STEER -- L/R are screen-relative WALK keys, not a rotation, so turn-then-go is meaningless on foot. FIX (the correct use of .79's camera-yaw disassembly, which .80 misused): steer in SCREEN space. screenAngle = (targetBearing - yaw) & 0xFFF is the target's direction relative to screen-up (= what UP walks); press the NEAREST OF 8 ARROW COMBOS (cardinals + diagonals: sector = ((screenAngle+256)>>9)&7, UP / UP+RIGHT / RIGHT / DOWN+RIGHT / DOWN / DOWN+LEFT / LEFT / UP+LEFT) so he walks toward the target and STAIRCASES along the road's bends. At the .86 wedge screenAngle ~= +318 (28deg CW of screen-up) -> sector 1 = UP+RIGHT -> walks NNE, AWAY from the cliff and along the road, instead of NNW into it. The on-foot branch is RE-SPLIT off the vehicle heading law (the dead if(false&&isOnFoot) screen-basis/probe block stays as dead code; a new else-if(isOnFoot) holds the yaw steering; vehicles keep the unchanged heading turn-then-go else). Routing/cursor/steer-target+LOS-clamp/stuck/replan/reverse-burst/final-approach ALL unchanged; the reverse-burst un-wedge (DOWN=screen-down) is kept for foot recovery. New [YAWDRIVE] trace logs yaw/tgtBrg/screenAngle/keys. ONE handedness assumption: RIGHT walks screen-right = yaw+90 CW; if the BAT shows him tracking bends the WRONG way (steering consistently to the wrong side at a turn), swap RIGHT<->LEFT (one line). BAT .87: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [YAWDRIVE] to press UP+RIGHT (not pure UP) at the start, Squall to walk NNE ALONG the road instead of NNW into the cliff, idx to CLIMB past 0, dist to fall past 19.5km, ideally ARRIVAL staircasing along the road; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- vehicles are untouched, but confirm foot still works on a road-less continent where the route is open ground). Watch: (a) handedness (wrong-way at a turn -> swap R/L); (b) do 2-key diagonals combine into diagonal movement (if not, he'll jerk between the two cardinals -- still nets toward target, but the fallback is time-multiplexed cardinal staircasing). If he now follows the road but stalls at a TIGHT bend, that's a local-geometry/lookahead tune, not the steering model. After a clean drive set all #67 diags false (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG/ROAD_MAP_DIAG) and push .54-onward as ONE commit; then BAT 3 (coord audit). // v0.18.3.86: #67 -- ROAD-PREFERENCE in the planner (build 3 of the road pivot; makes the route FOLLOW the road instead of leaving it). The .85 road-walkable override BAT SOLVED routing in principle (the planner now CAN route the road -- 623 road cells forced walkable, the ribbon reconnected) but the drive still wedged ~13.5km out at the START, oscillating, idx=0/20, never advancing. F11 at the wedge = Squall at the FOOT OF A COASTAL CLIFF, and the [ROADMAP]/[ROUTEMAP] showed why: the start fine(101,65) IS a road cell (row 65 cols 100-105 are road) and the road winds continuously NE up to Dollet, BUT the route LEFT the road at fine(101,63) -- which is LAND, not road -- where the road jogs EAST to cols 104-107; the planner cut straight up col 101 onto the non-road false-land cliff (a couple cells shorter than following the road's bend) and drove into the rock. ROOT: the clearance-weighted Dijkstra had NO road awareness -- it cut corners off the road wherever a non-road shortcut was shorter, straight into the false-land cliffs the road exists to avoid. FIX: road-preference in PlanPathFine's step cost -- a step onto a road cell (s_roadFine, already populated unconditionally since .84) costs 1 with NO clearance penalty (the road IS the corridor, needs no centering); a step onto a NON-road cell pays the clearance penalty PLUS a flat WM_OFFROAD_PENALTY=40, so any multi-cell off-road shortcut loses to staying on the road and the route FOLLOWS the road's bends to Dollet. Penalty is finite, so non-road terrain stays usable for the short start/end hops and on road-less continents (Balamb: with no road cells competing, every step pays the same flat penalty -> relative costs and the route are UNCHANGED -- no regression). This addresses the .81 lesson that road-attraction fights the clearance penalty by ZEROING the clearance term on road cells. NOTE the on-foot EXECUTOR is still .80's heading turn-then-go, which can't truly steer on foot (hdg=0x0203ED02 is the fixed per-region CAMERA YAW, not a controllable facing; the .85 trace showed it frozen at 3783 pressing U into the cliff) -- but at the .85 wedge BOTH the heading-law and a correct yaw-based steerer would press the same arrow (target ~16deg off screen-up), so the EXECUTOR was not the differentiator there; the ROUTE pointing into the cliff was. Once the route follows the road (real walkable ground), if the executor then mis-steers at the road's turns, the NEXT build is the yaw-based screen-relative steering (read the yaw at 0x0203ED02, arrow = nearest(targetBearing - yaw); replaces .80's turn-then-go on foot, vehicles keep the heading law) -- the correct use of the camera-yaw disassembly, which .80 misused as a steerable facing. BAT .86: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect the [ROUTEMAP] route to STAY on the road (follow the eastward jog at fine(101,63)->cols 104-107, NOT cut straight up col 101 into the cliff), the drive to actually progress past 13.5km along the road, and ideally ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- road-preference changes every planned route's cost, though road-less routes should be identical). If the route now hugs the road but the executor still oscillates at a road turn, that isolates the executor -> do the yaw-based steering next. After a clean drive set all #67 diags false (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG/ROAD_MAP_DIAG) and push .54-onward as ONE commit; then BAT 3 (coord audit). // v0.18.3.85: #67 -- ROAD-WALKABLE OVERRIDE (build 2 of the road pivot; the executor/planner now route on the reconnected road). The .84 [ROADMAP] dump CONFIRMED the road: terrain types 27/28 present (3605+1875 polys), 623 road fine cells, the ribbon reaches Dollet (R adjacent to D at fine(113,57)), and the Dollet-drive start fine(102,64) sits ON the road. It also revealed (a) WHY the planner kept hitting the cliff -- the road does NOT go straight up fine col 102; at rows 62-64 it jogs EAST to cols 104-107 and climbs to Dollet from there, while col 102 reads as walkable LAND in the grid but is the in-game cliff (and col 102 dead-ends into ocean at row 58, so straight-north was never a through path); and (b) that a large stretch of the road was being FORCE-BLOCKED by our own grid -- the [ROADMAP] '!' (road-over-blocked) cells cluster at rows 59-69 cols 104-111, EXACTLY the AABB the .81 Dollet false-coast patch blocks (the patch sits on top of the real road), plus a few road cells running through steep-mtn-classified cells. THIS build makes the road authoritative: after rasterization AND the .81 patch, every s_roadFine cell is forced to SEG_LAND steep=0 (walkable), overriding both the steep-mtn misclassification and the .81 patch wherever they fall on the road -- so the road becomes one continuous walkable ribbon from the start to Dollet, while non-road cells (incl. the rest of the false ledge) keep their classification. Applied BEFORE the clearance BFS so reachability/clearance/planner all see the road as the open corridor; through the cliff pinch the road is now the ONLY walkable path, so the existing clearance-weighted Dijkstra is funneled onto it with NO planner rewrite. New [ROADMAP] 'road-walkable override' log line reports how many road cells were forced (and how many had been blocked). The [ROADMAP] dump (still on) now renders the previously-'!' cells as 'R' = built-in confirmation. BAT .85: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect the [ROADMAP] override line (wasBlk>0, ~the .81 patch's road cells + steep-mtn road cells), the dump's '!' cells flipped to 'R', the [PLAN]/[ROUTEMAP] route to jog EAST onto the road around the col-102 cliff (not drive straight into it), dist past 12km, and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- road-forcing changes the grid, though Balamb's route is on a different continent). If the route now follows the road but the EXECUTOR wanders off it in the OPEN stretches (steering works on open ground per .66, so this is unlikely to block), build 3 adds an explicit road-preference/restriction to the planner. If he still wedges, the [HDG-DIAG]/[ROUTEMAP] show whether it's the route (still off the road) or the executor (on the road but mis-steering). After a clean drive set all #67 diags false (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG/ROAD_MAP_DIAG) and push .54-onward as ONE commit; then BAT 3 (coord audit). // v0.18.3.84: #67 -- ROAD extraction (load-only diagnostic; PIVOT from inferred-terrain steering to the road as ground-truth). Three steering BATs (.80/.82/.83) all wedged ~12km because the planner routes up col 102 into an in-game CLIFF that the 1024u grid mis-classifies as walkable LAND -- confirmed by F11 (Squall at the foot of a sheer rock face) + the trace (he walks freely north to the row-63/64 boundary then cannot cross; reverse frees him south; he never enters fine row 63). ROOT (answer to 'why do we keep hitting mountains'): the mod does NOT read true walkability -- it rasterizes wmx polygons into a coarse 1024u grid and GUESSES walkable from terrain-type + a steepness threshold (WM_MTN_STEEP_BLOCK) tuned for reachability not collision; both guesses fail at cliffs (a cliff face + the flat ground at its base land in adjacent cells; the rasterizer marks a cell land if any land poly touches it) and narrow passes. Aaron's fix (correct): the wmx tags ROAD polys (terrain 27/28 = Road/Railroad) -- the one signal both reliably walkable AND running exactly Timber->Dollet. Route ALONG the road instead of inferring terrain, and use the road as ground-truth to refine the grid (road cells = walkable; off-road 'land' cells wedged between the road and a mountain band are suspect cliff). THIS build is step 1, pure observation, ZERO behavior change: LoadTerrainGrid now captures road polys into a new s_roadFine[192][256] overlay (RasterizeTriRoad: centre-in-triangle PLUS the 3 vertex + centroid cells, so a sub-cell-thin ribbon still registers), tallies a terrain-type histogram, and (ROAD_MAP_DIAG) logs [ROADMAP]: road fine-cell count, the type:polys histogram (confirms 27/28 exist + how many), and an ASCII map of the Dollet corridor (rows 45..76 cols 88..121) with the road ribbon over the fine class grid (R=road, !=road-over-a-cell-we-currently-block, D=Dollet, ~/./f/m/^). BAT .84: load a GALBADIA world-map save (NO driving needed -- the dump fires once at terrain load), send Logs/ff8_world.log. Read [ROADMAP]: (1) histogram confirms road=27/28 present; (2) road fine cells > 0 and the ribbon forms a continuous chain to D; (3) any '!' = road cells we wrongly block. Build 2 then routes the Dollet drive ALONG s_roadFine + forces road cells walkable + flags suspect off-road cliff cells. ROAD_MAP_DIAG retires before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.83: #67 -- FORWARD-COLLISION GUARD on the steering law (a step-back redesign toward terrain-reactive execution). The .82 LOS-clamp WORKED (the steer target moved to the clamped near cell fine(102,63), confirmed in [HDG-DIAG] steer(-26112,-33280)) but he STILL wedged ~12km out at the SAME cliff corner, and the trace pinpointed the proximate cause: target NNW, hdg due-north 36, off=318 -- JUST inside the ~320 STEER_DEADZONE -- so the law chose STRAIGHT and walked him due north into the blocked fine(103,63) the route goes WEST around; the stuck-reverse backed him off then the unchanged law walked him right back (reverse->re-aim->re-wedge), hdg frozen at 36 the entire time (he never pivoted because off never exceeded the deadzone). Re-eval (Aaron-requested): the executor is an open-loop bearing-follower BLIND to the terrain directly in front of the character -- no target-selection tweak fixes that, because 'roughly aimed' within the deadzone can still be aimed into a wall. FIX (first step toward reactive local avoidance): before the final STRAIGHT branch commits UP, probe the fine cell ~1 cell ahead of the CURRENT facing (heading->world vector, one WM_FINE_CELL out); if it's blocked for foot/car (SEG_OCEAN or steep SEG_MOUNTAIN), set fwdGuard and press toward the target side (err sign: RIGHT if err>=0 else LEFT) instead of UP. On this region's screen-relative controls (hdg is the FIXED per-region camera yaw -- frozen at 36 through STRAIGHT/PIVOT/REVERSE incl. a 600u reverse, the .73 behavior, NOT .80's rotate-the-facing) that walks him sideways WEST into the open col-102 corridor; on a turn-then-go region it pivots him there. The guard reads the grid in front of him so it fires ONLY near a wall -- open-road steering + the deadzone are unchanged (no orbit/oscillation regression, preserves .60/.78). Reuses the existing pivot key choice; no new state, no planner/target change (the .82 clamp stays). [HDG-DIAG] gains a GUARD marker (a PIVOT line with off<=deadzone + GUARD = the guard fired). BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] at the corner to show GUARD with keys=--L- (or --R-) when the cell ahead is blocked, him to move WEST around the cliff instead of pinning on it, dist past 12km, ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- the guard is in every planned drive's steering, but only acts adjacent to a wall). If he moves west but then can't make the next turn, the next step is the full feeler-fan controller (probe several headings, pick the open one best toward target) + a blocked-direction memory for convergent recovery. If he STILL pins, the trace shows whether GUARD fired and which key -- tells us if the issue is the guard or the control model. After a clean BAT set all #67 diags false and push .54-onward as ONE commit. // v0.18.3.82: #67 -- TIGHT-CORNER navigation: clamp the steer target to a clear line of sight + give on-foot the REVERSE un-wedge. The .81 patch WORKED (route went up the west canyon, dist 15.8km->11km) but he wedged at a real cliff corner fine(103,64): the F11 shows him pressed against a rock massif with open grass to his WEST, and [HDG-DIAG] shows the steer target aimed NNW (-26112,-35328) straight ACROSS the blocked fine(103,63) cliff that the winding route goes AROUND, hdg frozen at 36 (due north), pos sawing east-west, idx stuck at 0/29. Root: (1) the far ~2400u lookahead target sat on the far side of a cliff corner, so he pressed north INTO the rock; on the world map's tank controls rotation needs forward motion, so nosed into terrain he could neither advance nor pivot out. (2) WORSE -- on-foot had NO working recovery: the .80 unify routed on-foot STEERING to the vehicle heading law (if (false && isOnFoot) dead-branched the screen-relative path) but left on-foot RECOVERY pointing at the SIDESTEP, whose only consumer is inside that dead branch -- so the stuck check logged 'SIDESTEP left' and pressed NOTHING. TWO complementary fixes (separable in the trace): (a) wire the pre-written FineLineClearFootCar clamp -- walk the lookahead target back to the farthest route cell reachable by a clear straight line, so he aims ALONG the open corridor (here due WEST around the cliff) and turns there in open ground BEFORE reaching the rock; engages only near walls (open ground keeps the far target -> no orbit, preserves .78). (b) on-foot stuck recovery now fires the SAME DOWN reverse-burst as vehicles (tank controls: only reverse frees a nosed-in character; a lateral sidestep can't, and DOWN moves him on foot per .71) -- backs him off into open ground where the pivot can rotate him around the corner. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] steer target to track the open corridor (NOT a fixed NNW point across a cliff), keys=---D reverse bursts if he does wedge, dist past 11km, ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this changes lookahead + recovery for every planned drive). If he still wedges: the trace shows whether the clamp moved the steer target (steerX/Y) and whether reverse fired (D key) -- tells us which half to push. After a clean BAT set all #67 diags false and push .54-onward as ONE commit. // v0.18.3.81: #67 -- HARDCODED Dollet false-coast no-walk patch (Aaron-approved one-off). After the .80 heading-unify BAT still wedged ~15.8km out at the east->north coastal corner, offline wmx.obj analysis found the ROOT cause is the PLANNER, not the executor: the route runs up a thin coastal cliff ledge SE of Dollet that reads as walkable LAND in wmx but is impassable on foot in-game, and NO geometric rule separates it from real land (validated offline across 7 rules -- blanket cliff-block AND coastal-ledge-block both DISCONNECT same-continent Dollet from the start; 512-res reconnects the road but breaks the Balamb Town->Fire Cavern regression; road-attraction reroutes only with the clearance penalty disabled and is parameter-fragile). The Timber->Dollet canyon road (wmx terrain types 27/28) one cell WEST is the real foot approach; the planner ignored it because the fine grid discards the road tag and the ledge looked more direct. Since Dollet is a one-off (coast on one side, canyon road on the other), LoadTerrainGrid now marks the ledge AABB impassable by COORDINATE (DOLLET_COAST_X0/X1/Y0/Y1 in world_map_state.inl), implemented as forced-steep MOUNTAIN (steep=0xFFFF) so the existing foot/car block rule (cls==SEG_MOUNTAIN && steep>WM_MTN_STEEP_BLOCK) catches it with ZERO change to IsFineTraversable; Garden/Ragnarok bypass the fine grid so are unaffected. Offline-validated against the LIVE clearance-weighted planner cost: with the patch the route runs WEST up the canyon to Dollet (not north up the coast) and Dollet stays reachable; Balamb is untouched (different cells, different continent). BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [TERRAIN] 'Dollet false-coast patch: blocked N fine cells', the [ROUTEMAP] route to run west/up the canyon rather than north up the coast, dist to drop past 15.8km, and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this changes the Galbadia-area grid only, but confirm Balamb destinations still route). If it arrives, set all #67 diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) false and push the .54-onward chain as one commit. // v0.18.3.80: #67 -- UNIFY on-foot steering onto the heading-based turn-then-go (the vehicle law). The v0.18.3.79 [YAWPROBE] BAT was decisive and POSITIVE: with the character actually walking (footsteps confirmed), holding UP walked a STRAIGHT line whose COMPASS bearing == the camera yaw (0x0203ED02), matched within 1deg at TWO different yaws in the SAME run (the camera rotated mid-run: yaw 316deg -> walked 315deg; yaw 355deg -> walked 356deg); holding UP did NOT rotate the camera (yaw steady through each forward burst); and RIGHT INCREASED the yaw (clockwise). So 0x0203ED02 is the LIVE on-foot facing -- NOT frozen (the .72/.73 'frozen' reads were the character wedged/pressing UP into terrain, which doesn't rotate) and NOT screen-relative-per-region. The relationship has ZERO offset in the game's own bearing units: desiredYaw == the target's TorusBearing (0=N, CW), exactly what the existing VEHICLE steering already computes (err=TorusBearing-heading; if |off|>STEER_DEADZONE pivot RIGHT when err>=0 since RIGHT raises the yaw, else LEFT; else UP) and which already works for cars. FIX: the on-foot executor branch (the .74-.78 screen-basis + .77 greedy arrow-probe, built on the now-disproven frozen-heading reading) is BYPASSED -- gated `if (false && isOnFoot)`, left in place as dead code like the retired DCAL calibration -- so on foot now falls through to the SAME heading turn-then-go as vehicles. No other behavior change; routing/cursor/waypoint/stuck/replan/sweep/reverse-burst/final-approach all unchanged. The on-foot [HDG-DIAG] trace (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG) now logs hdg/tgtBrg/err/off/keys/band for the foot drive. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] hdg to track tgtBrg, err to shrink as he aligns then STRAIGHT frames carrying him along the route, dist dropping past 15.8km, and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this routes foot through the previously vehicle-only branch). If he wedges at a tight corner and can't pivot out (the old .66-.72 failure mode: turning needs a little open space), that is now a GEOMETRY/recovery issue, not a steering-law issue -- the clearance router already avoids tight corners and the reverse-burst un-wedge exists; tune those, do NOT revert to the probe. Set all #67 diags false before the push. // v0.18.3.79: #67 -- DIAGNOSTIC build, no steering change. Offline FF8_EN.exe disassembly (2026-06-22) located the world-map CAMERA: 0x0203ED02 = camera YAW (0..4096 = 360deg; live value 501 ~= 44deg), fed into the view-matrix builder at .text 0x552D20 (PSX RotMatrix/ApplyMatrix lib), SET on region transitions (writers 0x548155/0x54A1C8/0x54D030) and eased toward a target by a follow routine at 0x558590 (<=16 units/frame); 0x0203FE52 = camera PITCH (~45deg downward tilt). KEY REFRAME: the mod has been reading 0x0203ED02 ALL ALONG as GetWorldMapHeading() and wrongly dismissing it as a frozen, useless 'character heading' -- it is the CAMERA YAW, frozen on foot because the on-foot world-map camera holds a FIXED yaw PER REGION, which is exactly why holding one arrow walks a STRAIGHT line whose WORLD direction differs by region (Aaron-confirmed). This supersedes the entire heading/basis/probe steering arc (.66-.78): with the yaw readable, navigation needs no empirical basis and no orbit-prone probe -- read the yaw each frame, rotate the world->target bearing by -yaw, press the nearest arrow, re-read on region change. This build REPLACES the F12 diagnostic body only (world_map_camera_scan.inl, SAME external symbols TriggerCameraScan/UpdateCameraScan/CamScanPause/CamScanResume so dinput8.cpp + Poll() need no rewiring): retired the dead .73 G/H camera-rotate scan; new [YAWPROBE] holds UP ~1.2s (sampling pos+yaw every 250ms), then RIGHT, then UP again, logging the net world vector per hold + the computed offset(UP-walk bearing vs yaw) + turn sense. It CONFIRMS the yaw drives movement and MEASURES the one constant the steering rewrite needs. BAT: on the world map standing STILL (not driving), press F12, hold still ~6s (fully automated, TTS on start/complete; if a random encounter interrupts, fight it and the probe auto-restarts), send Logs/ff8_world.log; then WALK into the next region and press F12 again -- in the [YAWPROBE] SUMMARY the yaw should DIFFER between regions but offsetUP should MATCH (proves yaw-driven movement) and sense(RIGHT-UP) should be ~+/-90. No steering/routing change this build. Next build = the yaw-based steering rewrite using the measured offset. CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG + the remaining #67 diags retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.78: #67 -- fix the greedy-probe ORBIT by aiming at a FAR route target instead of the adjacent cell. The .77 BAT (greedy arrow-probe, built+run) proved the PROBE STEERING WORKS -- it correctly presses cardinals and moves him on open ground -- but he STILL hung ~15.8km out, and the trace + F11 screenshot revealed why, and it's NOT a wall: the F11 shows him standing on open green grass with a clear field to the north (cliffs/Dollet road far in the background), and the [SRDIAG] trace shows idx=0/22 the WHOLE time (cursor never advances past the first route cell) with tgtBrg swinging wildly (231->255->329->152->...) every quarter-second. That swing is the signature of ORBITING: the steer target was the IMMEDIATELY ADJACENT route cell (DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS=1, ~1024u away), so every ~180u walk step swung the bearing to it ~15deg, the probe re-decided every 250ms window, and he circled the first waypoint on row 70 instead of committing to a direction and crossing north into the open field. FIX: aim the steer target at the first route cell at least DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST (~2400u) ahead along the path (the OLD pre-.64 rule), not the adjacent cell. A ~2400u target swings only ~4deg per step, so the bearing is STABLE and the probe locks onto the northward cardinal and carries him up the corridor, advancing the cursor. The far target was cut to 1 cell back in .64 ONLY because the THEN heading-pivot steering drove straight at a far target and jammed into walls -- the greedy probe does NOT jam (it rejects walled cardinals via the <MIN_MOVE test and staircases around obstacles), so a far target is now both SAFE and NECESSARY to stop the orbit. One change; the probe logic, cursor advance, stuck/replan, sidestep, and vehicle path are all unchanged. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- in [SRDIAG] expect tgtBrg to now hold ROUGHLY STEADY (not swing 100s of units every line), idx to CLIMB past 0 as he advances up the route, dist to drop past 15.8km, and him to walk north across the open field toward the canyon instead of circling; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- this changes every planned drive's target selection). If he still orbits, raise DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST further or lower DRIVE_PROBE_ALIGN_MIN so the probe will take wider detours; if he commits to a direction but jams against real geometry, that's a grid/route issue at that cell. Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.77: #67 -- PIVOT the on-foot steering executor off the maintained screen->world basis to a GREEDY EMPIRICAL ARROW PROBE. Three BATs (.74 stale basis / .75 corrupt basis / .76 guard-frozen basis) all produced the SAME outcome: clean eastward progress to ~15.8km then sawing east-west at the route corner, never turning north -- which per the project's own rule (3 identical outcomes = wrong diagnosis, pivot) means the maintained-basis approach is the wrong tool. The .76 trace was decisive: even with the guard HOLDING the basis stable at uHat~(1,0), pressing the basis-predicted U-L- combo drove him WEST (basis predicted NNE) and he oscillated dist 15887<->17404 on row 70, never going north. A predicted screen->world basis is unreliable exactly where the camera swings (the corner), and 2-key diagonals don't combine as the basis assumes. The route itself is FINE (planner threads the gap; DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS=1 aims at the adjacent next cell) -- the executor just can't convert 'go toward that cell' into the right arrow. NEW APPROACH: trust ONLY measured progress. Hold ONE cardinal arrow for DRIVE_PROBE_WINDOW_MS(250); measure the window's displacement; KEEP the arrow if it both moved him (>=DRIVE_PROBE_MIN_MOVE=40u) AND lined up with the target dir (align >= DRIVE_PROBE_ALIGN_MIN=0.55, ~within 57deg); else ROTATE to the next cardinal (UP->RIGHT->DOWN->LEFT). No basis, no trig, no diagonals -- immune to camera swing and wall-slide (it only believes what it measures). At the corner this naturally STAIRCASES: the walled direction fails the MIN_MOVE test and it takes the open cardinal that actually progresses toward the next route cell. DRIVE_PROBE_MAX_FAILS(8) non-progressing windows in a row = genuine pocket -> re-plan. Calibration (the UP/RIGHT probe) is DISARMED (its basis is now unused); RefreshBasisAxis/OrthonormalizeAgainst/the basis state are now dead (left in place, like prior unused helpers). Cursor/steer-target/sidestep/stuck/replan/vehicle path all UNCHANGED. [SRDIAG] now logs arrow=UP/RIGHT/DOWN/LEFT + fails count instead of uHat/rHat. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- expect [SRDIAG] to settle on a single arrow that drops dist, STAIRCASE through the corner (arrow flips between two cardinals as it rounds it) WITHOUT the backward rout, and progress past 15.8km; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression). If he still can't round the corner, the trace shows which arrows the probe tried and whether ANY progressed (if none, it's a true terrain pocket -> grid/route fix; if one did but he didn't follow, tune the window/align). Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.76: #67 -- add a SLIDE-REJECTION guard to the screen-relative basis refresh. The .75 BAT was real progress: the refresh fix WORKED on open ground -- uHat/rHat tracked smoothly (they were frozen in .74) and he made the best on-foot progress yet, dist 16754->15807 (~950u) following the route east then starting north. But at the route corner he stalled against a blocked cell and then exactly the predicted wall-slide corruption hit: uHat rotated ~90deg from (-0.05,-1.00) to (1.00,-0.09) as the per-tick refresh learned from wall-slide/jitter motion (his real motion follows the wall, not the pressed arrow), and the corrupted basis then drove him BACKWARD -- dist climbed 15807->17404, farther out than he started. FIX: only refine the basis when the observed motion AGREES with the pressed arrow's predicted screen direction (dot > DRIVE_BASIS_AGREE_MIN=0.50, ~within 60deg) -- gentle camera tracking on open ground always agrees (~0.95) and passes; perpendicular/reversed wall-slide motion disagrees and is rejected, preserving the good basis. No other behavior change; threshold/Gram-Schmidt/calibration/decision/sidestep/vehicle path all unchanged. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- in [SRDIAG] uHat/rHat should track smoothly on open ground AND now HOLD steady (not swing ~90deg) when he contacts a wall, dist should keep dropping past 15.8km without the backward rout, and he should reach the route corner with a correct basis. Watch: (a) does uHat stay sane through the corner now; (b) does he still STALL at the corner (the .75 trace showed him nosing north into a blocked cell ~fine(104,69)=^ before the route's eastward leg -- if so the next fix is path-following/corner, NOT the basis) or does the sidestep round it; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression). Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.75: #67 -- FIX the screen-relative steering's dead basis-refresh (root cause of the .74 jam). The .74 BAT [SRDIAG] trace showed the approach WORKING at first (dist 15337->15028 while moving ~186u/frame) then wedging at the same ~15km coastal neck, and the smoking gun: uHat/rHat NEVER changed across the entire 15km drive (logged uHat(0.35,-0.93) rHat(0.93,0.36) on every line). The live basis-refresh was gated by DRIVE_BASIS_REFRESH_MIN=40, but per-tick walk motion is only ~15u (186u per 200ms / ~12 ticks), so refresh NEVER fired -- the basis stayed frozen at the drive-start camera angle while the world camera swung over 15km, so by the neck 'press UP' no longer meant north and steering drove him into the coast (keys=U--- forever, d+3-5, tgtBrg drifting). FIX: DRIVE_BASIS_REFRESH_MIN 40->10 so the basis tracks actual per-tick walking motion (still excludes wedge jitter <1u/tick); and after refreshing one axis from single-arrow motion, Gram-Schmidt the OTHER axis perpendicular to it (new OrthonormalizeAgainst) so the frame rotates rigidly with the camera and can't collapse (steering presses UP most of the time, so only uHat would refresh otherwise). No other behavior change; calibration, decision, sidestep, vehicle path all unchanged. BAT: on-foot drive to Dollet -- in [SRDIAG] expect uHat/rHat to now MOVE smoothly as he drives (tracking the camera) and dist to keep dropping past 15km; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression). Watch uHat in the trace: if it tracks smoothly and he progresses, the stale-basis bug is fixed; if instead uHat swings WILDLY when he slides along a wall, the next step is a slide-rejection guard (only refine when observed motion agrees with the basis). Diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.74: #67 -- REBUILD on-foot world-map steering as SCREEN-RELATIVE self-calibrating control (the real fix the .73 diagnostic pointed to). The .73 [CAMDIAG] BAT was decisive and NEGATIVE for the camera approach: injecting the manual's world-map camera keys (G=CCW, H=CW) did NOTHING -- across all 5 H taps and 5 G taps the hdng(0x0203ED00)/save(0x01CFFEB8) windows never changed and WM_HEADING stayed frozen at 3595; the only foot-window changes were the residual of the preceding UP-walk settling, not a per-tap camera ramp. So G/H don't rotate the camera in this build and there's no camera-angle field to read. BUT the scan confirmed the walk basis is clean and stable: UP-walk measured (-286,-286) three times (NW here), vs (+132,-132)=NE at the Dollet jam -- i.e. on foot the arrows WALK screen-relative and the screen->world mapping differs by region (the world camera faces different ways in different places), which is exactly why every heading-based steering attempt failed. FIX (foot drives only; vehicles keep the heading law): UpdateAutoDrive now MEASURES the screen->world basis from the character's own motion and presses the arrow combo whose screen direction points at the target. At drive start it calibrates -- press UP ~320ms -> world delta = screen-up unit vector uHat; settle; press RIGHT ~320ms -> screen-right unit vector rHat (retries up to 3x; perpendicular fallback if a probe can't move, corrected by live refresh) -- logged [SRDIAG] CAL. Then each tick it decomposes the wrapped world vector to the steer target onto (uHat,rHat): press UP if the screen-up component > cos(67.5deg), DOWN if < -that, RIGHT/LEFT likewise (8-way, diagonals allowed), and refreshes uHat/rHat live from any single-arrow motion (EMA) so the basis tracks the camera as it swings across the map. No heading, no pivot, no camera control, no reverse-into-loop. The on-foot stuck recovery is now a SIDESTEP (slide laterally past an obstacle, alternating sides) on the first stuck window, then the existing mid-route re-plan; vehicles keep the DOWN reverse burst. ALL routing/arrival/sweep/planner-eligibility scaffolding is UNCHANGED (the route was always fine; only the executor was broken). New state + constants in world_map_state.inl (DriveCalPhase, s_camU*/s_camR*, sidestep, DRIVE_CAL_*/STEER_AXIS_C/DRIVE_BASIS_*); helpers WrapWorldDelta + RefreshBasisAxis in world_map_drive.inl. Throttled [SRDIAG] trace logs pos/d+moved/tgtBrg/up+rt components/uHat/rHat/keys/SIDESTEP. BAT: on the world map on foot, select a far destination (Dollet) and press \ to auto-drive -- expect a brief UP-then-RIGHT calibration wobble at the start ([SRDIAG] CAL lines), then [SRDIAG] frames where up/rt pick the arrows toward the target and d+ stays POSITIVE (real progress, no eternal loop), and ARRIVAL; ALSO a Balamb on-foot drive (regression -- simple-coord, planner-ineligible, so only sidestep recovery). Read [SRDIAG]: if uHat/rHat look sane and the chosen arrows drive him toward tgtBrg, it's working; an inverted axis sign would show immediately as wrong arrows vs tgtBrg and is a one-line fix. The remaining #67 diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG/CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.73: #67 -- DIAGNOSTIC build, no steering change yet. The v0.18.3.72 BAT trace was decisive: WM_HEADING (0x0203ED02) is FROZEN at 501 across the whole jam -- unchanged through PIVOT, STRAIGHT and REVERSE frames AND while he walked 600+ units in every direction during the DOWN bursts -- so it is NOT the on-foot facing, and the entire closed-loop heading-pivot steering (.66-.72) has been steering against a dead constant. On-foot world-map movement is DIRECTIONAL WALK (arrows walk screen-relative; measured from the trace: UP -> world (+132,-132), DOWN -> world (-132,+138), camera tilted ~45deg). UP walks him NE into the east coast -> pins; stuck-check fires DOWN -> walks him SW into open land; reverse ends -> UP walks him back to the same pin -> eternal loop. Reverse was never going to fix a steering loop that can't see its facing. Aaron's direction: have the mod OWN the camera (G/H rotate it -- confirmed via the FF8 PC manual: G=counter-clockwise, H=clockwise, NOT the Q/E first guessed) so it can both perceive AND control orientation. This build replaces the F12 diagnostic: RETIRED world_map_heading_scan.inl (HEADING_SCAN_DIAG 0 -- it injected RIGHT/LEFT, which are WALK keys on foot, so it rotated nothing and never found a facing) and added world_map_camera_scan.inl (CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG 1). F12 (dinput8.cpp) now calls WorldMap::TriggerCameraScan. The new scan, fully automated while the player holds still (~5s, TTS on start + completion, pause/resume around encounters): measures a short UP-walk vector (A), taps H x5 (clockwise) sampling 3 memory windows (foot 0x0203EE00 / hdng 0x0203ED00 / save 0x01CFFEB8) after each tap, measures UP-walk (B), taps G x5 (counter-clockwise), measures UP-walk (C). Reads from [CAMDIAG] in ff8_world.log: (1) the window offset whose b-delta ramps through the H taps and reverses through the G taps = the camera-angle field we can perceive; (2) whether the A/B/C UP-walk vectors rotate = movement is camera-relative; (3) per-tap rotation from B vs A. Decides the architecture: if G/H rotate the camera and a value tracks it, the real fix locks the camera to a canonical angle so UP always means a known world direction; else fall back to the empirically-derived walk basis with direct screen-relative arrow steering (like F9 field nav). BAT: on the world map standing still (NOT driving), press F12, hold still ~5s (you may hear a Gil readout from the G taps -- expected; if an encounter interrupts, fight it and it auto-restarts), then send Logs/ff8_world.log. No steering/routing behavior change this build. CAMERA_SCAN_DIAG + the remaining #67 diags (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/WM_CALIB_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.72: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- make the reverse un-wedge ACTUALLY FIRE. The .71 BAT proved it still never fired, and the log showed exactly why: instead of a reverse burst, every mid-route stuck check fell through to '[DRIVE] Stuck mid-route -- re-planning ... recovery 5/8, 6/8' (a futile re-plan that recomputes the identical 21-cell route every time). Cause: the reverse had a separate 4-use budget (MAX_WEDGE_REVERSE) that got spent early in the drive and NEVER reset -- each random encounter (~every few seconds here) resumes him on the same neck and re-plans but does NOT reset the budget (only StartAutoDrive does, and the progress watermark only renews on getting closer, which never happens), so after 4 bursts the reverse was permanently locked out for the rest of the drive. FIX: drop the separate 4-use budget; fire the reverse on EVERY mid-route stuck check while there is still give-up headroom (s_driveStuckCount < DRIVE_STUCK_MAX), and let stuckCount keep climbing so it still gives up after DRIVE_STUCK_MAX if reversing never helps. stuckCount resets on resume, so each post-encounter segment gets a fresh set of attempts -- the reverse can no longer be starved. Still DOWN-only; the plan is DOWN backs him off the wall into open ground, then the normal PIVOT re-aims him (rotation appears to need open space -- turning in place while pinned does nothing, which is why the heading stays frozen at the wedge). NOTE: the route is confirmed FINE (the .71 [ROUTEMAP] again showed the single open corridor between ocean and blocked mountains). NOTE 2: random encounters are firing every few seconds at this spot, chopping the drive into short segments -- a separate concern, but segments are usually long enough for at least one 3s stuck check. BAT: drive to Dollet -- at the jam expect '[DRIVE] Stuck -> reverse un-wedge burst (check N/6)' then [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames with a D key; the decisive reads are (1) does his pos/dist SHIFT on the REVERSE frames (DOWN moves him) and (2) once he's backed off, does hdg finally start CHANGING as the PIVOT re-aims him (rotation works in open space) -- if both, he should round the corner and continue. If reverse moves him but he still can't rotate after backing off, that's the next thing to chase. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.71: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- REVERSE un-wedge, now actually triggered. Aaron confirmed pressing DOWN moves the character on the world map, so the reverse primitive is sound; the bug was that the recovery NEVER FIRED in the .68/.69/.70 BATs because it was keyed on a hard-wedge detector (no-movement in .68, net-displacement-250u in .69) that the wall-oscillation kept defeating. The .70 clearance change also had no effect on the route -- the [ROUTEMAP] showed it threading the ONE open north-south corridor between the ocean (east) and a band of blocked mountain cells (west); col 105 is already the clearance-optimal lane, so a stronger bias had nothing better to move to. Routing is fine; the blocker is purely the start-cell wedge (he resumes on a narrow water/cliff neck, hdg frozen 254, can't turn out -- a full second of LEFT-only never moved the heading -- and 'forward' slides him along the waterline). FIX: trigger the reverse burst from the PROVEN stuck-check (it logs 'Stuck check N/6' reliably every 3s) instead of the dead hard-wedge path, and make the burst DOWN-only (no simultaneous turn -- turning while wedged does nothing and muddied .68/.69; a clean backward push into open ground, then normal steering re-aims). On a mid-route stuck check (dist>=DRIVE_FINAL_APPROACH_DIST, budget MAX_WEDGE_REVERSE left) it sets s_driveWedgeReverseUntil=now+REVERSE_BURST_MS, logs '[DRIVE] Stuck -> reverse un-wedge burst N/4', resets the give-up counter, and returns so DOWN is pressed from the next tick; the progress watermark renews the budget once a reverse gets him >=WEDGE_PROGRESS_EPS closer, else it falls through to re-plan/give-up. BAT: drive to Dollet -- at the jam expect '[DRIVE] Stuck -> reverse un-wedge burst 1/4' then [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames with a D key; the decisive read is whether d+ goes POSITIVE and his position/dist actually shifts on those frames (reverse frees him -> deadlock broken, tune from there) or stays ~0 (something still blocks it). If he backs off and then re-aims and continues, that's the win. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.70: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- CLEARANCE-PREFERRING PLANNER (Aaron-requested): prioritize an open, clean path over minimizing distance. The .69 BAT (wedge detector still didn't fire -- this run the oscillation amplitude was ~840u E-W, far above WEDGE_NET_EPS=250, so the net-displacement anchor kept resetting) came with F11 screenshots that finally showed the real problem: the player is sawing east-west along a thin SHORELINE strip at the water's edge with a WIDE-OPEN grass field right beside him -- the route hugs the coast when open ground is adjacent, and 'forward' just slides him along the waterline (hdg frozen 7, Y pinned ~-26360, X swinging 840u, dist stuck ~15km). That is a routing problem, not a wedge-recovery problem: the route should go through the open grass, not along the water. PlanPathFine already biases toward clearance (s_clearFine = Chebyshev dist to nearest ocean/steep-mountain), but far too weakly (WM_CLEAR_PENALTY=6, WM_CLEAR_TARGET=3 made a wall/water-adjacent cell cost only 13 vs 1 open). Raised to WM_CLEAR_PENALTY=20, WM_CLEAR_TARGET=5: a clearance-1 (edge-hugging) cell now costs ~81 vs 1 for open ground (clearance>=5), so the Dijkstra detours FAR to stay clear of water/mountain edges and only dips to low clearance at the unavoidable coastal destination itself. Reachability is unchanged (costs stay finite; IsFineTraversable untouched) -- only the cost weighting changed, so a genuinely narrow-only corridor is still taken, just at high cost. Tunable; the [ROUTEMAP] dump at plan time shows the new route immediately. BAT: drive to Dollet -- check [ROUTEMAP] in ff8_world.log shows the route now running through OPEN ground (inland) rather than along the coast, and the player should stay on open grass where the steering already works (it drove 19->17km smoothly on open ground in .66) and ARRIVE; ALSO a Balamb drive (regression -- the stronger bias changes every route). If he still pins on the new open route, next step is the progress-based wedge detector (the .68/.69 reverse recovery never got to fire -- DOWN is still untested). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.69: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- wedge detector FIX: key the hard-wedge on NET displacement, not raw movement. The .68 BAT proved the reverse un-wedge NEVER fired -- no [DRIVE] burst line, no D key the whole run -- because the jam is a wall-VIBRATION, not stillness: the player bounces ~60u east-west every frame (per-frame d+ 30-62) while netting ~zero forward progress (dist pinned at 14960), and those bounces kept resetting the .68 move-timer so the hard-wedge (no-movement for HARD_WEDGE_MS) never tripped. The mod's own slow stuck-check DID see it (moved 6/9 units in 3000ms) but that only feeds give-up. Fix: detect the wedge by NET displacement from an anchor that only resets when he genuinely relocates WEDGE_NET_EPS=250u, so vibration-in-place trips hardWedge after HARD_WEDGE_MS and the reverse finally fires. Secondary finding (informs .70): pressing LEFT to turn while MOVING 60+ units did NOT rotate him (hdg frozen at 201 all run), so turning is dead while jammed -- relocating via reverse is the needed escape, and this BAT is the decisive test of whether DOWN does anything on the world map. BAT: drive to Dollet -- at the corner expect [DRIVE] 'Hard wedge -> reverse un-wedge burst N/4' + [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames with a D key; READ whether d+ (and especially the net pos change) goes POSITIVE on those frames. If yes, reverse works -> tune from there. If d+~0 on REVERSE too, DOWN is inert on the world map -> .70 attacks it from routing. Next (.70, Aaron-requested): clearance-preferring planner -- bias the route toward open ground over shortest distance so it avoids pinch corners in the first place. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG/ROUTE_MAP_DIAG/HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.68: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- REVERSE un-wedge recovery. The .67 route-map dump answered Aaron's routing question: the route is directionally correct (NE to Dollet) but runs along the coast for the north leg (the inland is mountain-broken in the grid), and the recurring 15km jam is at the east->north CORNER against the coast. The trace pinned the mechanism: on the world map the character only ROTATES while moving forward, so when he noses into the coast at the corner he can't move, can't turn, and his heading FREEZES (hdg stuck at 501 the whole jam) -- he just presses forward into the same water forever. The closed-loop steering itself works (drove 19km->17km smoothly on the open leg before a random encounter); what was missing is wedge recovery. Fix: add a DOWN-arrow (reverse) injection + a wedge-recovery branch in UpdateAutoDrive. On a HARD wedge (no real movement for HARD_WEDGE_MS=1200, longer than the WALL_SLIDE_MS slide window), reverse off the wall for REVERSE_BURST_MS=600 while turning toward the target (back-and-aim) to regain open ground + motion, then resume normal steering. Bounded to MAX_WEDGE_REVERSE=4 bursts per wedge; the budget renews only on genuine progress (got WEDGE_PROGRESS_EPS=500u closer), so a true no-progress jam still falls through to the existing stuck-detection / re-plan / give-up instead of reversing forever. Also dropped the counterproductive wall-jam forced-RIGHT nudge (it shoved him away from a left-turn target). Key injection: SetDriveKeys gains a 4th 'down' param (VK_DOWN 0x28 / scan 0x50, extended); ReleaseAllDriveKeys releases it. [HDG-DIAG] gains a REVERSE band + a D key column. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- at the coastal corner expect [DRIVE] 'Hard wedge -> reverse un-wedge burst N/4' lines and [HDG-DIAG] REVERSE frames where d+ goes POSITIVE (he backs off) and hdg starts changing again, then him rounding the corner and continuing. If reverse moves him (d+>0 on REVERSE frames) the deadlock is broken even if tuning is needed; if d+ stays ~0 on REVERSE too, DOWN does nothing on the world map and we need a different un-wedge. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG / ROUTE_MAP_DIAG / HEADING_SCAN_DIAG retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.67: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- DIAGNOSTIC: route-map dump to verify the planner actually picks the right path to Dollet. The .66 closed-loop steering BAT jammed ~15km out on a coastal spit (F11 screenshot: tall cliff NW, ocean E, narrow shoreline strip), and [HDG-DIAG] showed hdg FROZEN at 502 the whole time while keys cycled U/L/R -- he could not rotate out. But the deeper question (Aaron) is whether the ROUTE is even correct: you reach Dollet via an inland canyon, not by hugging the coast, and the log only prints the route's endpoints (21 cells fine(105,70)->fine(112,57)), never the intermediate cells, so neither of us can tell if the path threads the canyon or scrapes the shore. New DumpRouteMap (gated ROUTE_MAP_DIAG, called in PlanPathFine at plan time) logs an ASCII map of the REAL fine terrain grid spanning the planned route with the route overlaid (.=land f=forest m=gentle-mtn ~=ocean; CAPS/X/^=blocked for this vehicle; S=start D=Dollet G=goal o=route; row 0=north, col 0=west), so we can SEE whether the chosen path is the canyon or a coast-hug AND whether the grid's walkable/blocked classification is even right. No steering/routing behavior change. BAT: start a Dollet auto-drive -- the [ROUTEMAP] block logs IMMEDIATELY at plan time, so no need to drive into the jam; just send Logs/ff8_world.log. ROUTE_MAP_DIAG (plus DRIVE_STEER_DIAG + HEADING_SCAN_DIAG) retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.66: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- REBUILD steering as closed-loop control on the REAL facing. The .65 F12 heading scan was decisive: WM_HEADING (0x0203ED02, exactly what GetWorldMapHeading already reads) IS the live facing -- holding RIGHT raised it ~80/sample (3595->4051->wrap->99, clockwise), holding LEFT reversed it, in the SAME compass units as TorusBearing (0=N, clockwise). The earlier 'frozen' DIAGNOSIS was wrong in its conclusion: the value wasn't dead, the character simply wasn't rotating during those drives -- he was jammed pressing forward+turn into terrain, which the engine won't rotate, so his facing genuinely stayed at his resting value (3595 in .62-.64, 540 in .61 = just each save's start facing). The motion-derived workaround then layered wall-slide corruption on top. Fix: UpdateAutoDrive now steers closed-loop on GetWorldMapHeading with a TURN-THEN-GO law -- err = targetBearing - heading (wrapped to [-2048,2048]); when |err| > STEER_DEADZONE, PIVOT turn-only (RIGHT if err>=0 since RIGHT raises the heading, else LEFT; NO forward, so the engine rotates him freely and the heading updates -- exactly the scan's clean turn-only phase); once aligned, drive forward; within FINAL_APPROACH_FORWARD_DIST just walk straight; a wall-jam watchdog forces a pivot if he stops moving while aimed. A true heading lets the pivot stop the instant we're aligned (kills the oscillation), and turn-only sidesteps the arc-into-wall jam that locked rotation before. ALL routing (clearance Dijkstra + path lookahead) is reused UNCHANGED -- this is the steering law only, general, not per-town. The motion-derived heading machinery (s_driveMoveHeading + runtime turn-sign calibration + duty-cycle) is gone from the steering path. New throttled [HDG-DIAG] trace logs the real heading vs target bearing + err + keys to confirm it tracks during a live drive. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [HDG-DIAG] hdg to rotate toward tgtBrg while PIVOTing, err to shrink and stay small on STRAIGHT frames, and him to thread the canyon and ARRIVE; also a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG and the F12 heading scan (HEADING_SCAN_DIAG) both retire before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.65: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- STEP BACK from steering tuning to a live-facing DISCOVERY diagnostic. Ten builds of steering work all fought one missing sensor: we cannot read which way the character is FACING. WM_HEADING (0x0203ED02) is frozen -- the .61 diagnostic proved it returns a constant all session -- so every steering decision since has run on a motion-derived heading that lags the turn and is corrupted whenever he slides along a wall (his motion then follows the wall, not his facing): the source of the .63/.64 oscillation-then-wedge. The real facing DOES exist in memory (FFNx's world.cpp reads the live camera out of sub_4023D0; the savemap WORLDMAP struct carries a rotation field) -- we just have the wrong address. New world_map_heading_scan.inl finds it deterministically and blind-accessibly: on the world map, press F12 -- while the player holds still it INJECTS a known turn (RIGHT ~1.6s then LEFT ~1.6s, turn-only via SetDriveKeys(0,0,1)/(0,1,0) so he pivots roughly in place, no forward/gas) and dumps three candidate memory windows (foot@0x0203EE00 256B, hdng@0x0203ED00 64B, save@0x01CFFEB8 32B) interpreted as int16 words every 150ms, logging each changed word's per-sample (s) and per-baseline (b) delta under [HSCAN]. The live facing is the offset whose s-delta holds ONE sign across the RIGHT samples and FLIPS across the LEFT samples (frame counters ramp the same sign both phases; position words move only if he translated). Announces \"Heading scan complete\" on finish; pauses and resumes around random encounters via Poll()'s existing world-map exit/entry detection (HScanPause/HScanResume -- the same mechanism auto-drive uses), re-snapshotting the baseline and restarting the interrupted turn phase on resume. F12 wired in dinput8.cpp gated on !alt (it was the free per-session diagnostic key; no old F12 handler to retire). No steering/routing change this build. BAT: on the world map standing still, press F12, let it run ~4s (if an encounter interrupts, fight it -- the scan announces \"paused\" then \"resuming\" and continues), then send Logs/ff8_world.log; the [HSCAN] block reveals the live facing address, which the next build rebuilds steering around as proper closed-loop control (no motion-derivation, no wall-corruption, no oscillation). All routing is reused as-is; this is NOT route-hardcoding. // v0.18.3.64: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- damp the steering oscillation + tighten the lookahead. The .63 BAT was real progress: sign=+1 held, the player drove the RIGHT direction and covered ~3km toward Dollet (19km->16km), then PINNED at a tight canyon bend, oscillating in place -- [DRIVE-DIAG] showed his motion heading swinging ~180deg past the target and back every frame (mh 2749<->746, keys U-R<->UL-), d+ ~2-6, dist stuck at 16446, until the stuck-check fired. Two causes: (1) bang-bang turning with no damping -- holding the turn key every frame over-rotates ~180deg past the target, then reverses and overshoots back; (2) the 3-cell lookahead again aimed at a point across the canyon wall, so he mashed into it and his motion-derived heading read the wall-deflected motion. Two fixes: aim at the NEXT path cell only (DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS 3->1) -- consecutive BFS path cells are 4-adjacent and walkable, so the straight line to the target can never cross a wall; and DUTY-CYCLE the turn (TURN_DUTY_ON_MS 60 / OFF 120, ~33%) so straight forward frames between turn pulses let the heading catch up and he converges instead of spinning. Full turn still applies while wall-jammed. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [DRIVE-DIAG] err to actually SHRINK and stay small (not swing +1600/-450), d+ to stay positive (forward progress), dist to keep dropping past 16km to arrival; also a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG -> false before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.63: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 follow-up to the .62 root-cause fix. The .62 BAT was the breakthrough -- the motion-derived heading made the player MOBILE for the first time (he traversed the continent; mh tracked his motion while the raw read stayed frozen at a constant 3595) -- but he drove to Galbadia Garden instead of Dollet. The [DRIVE-DIAG] trace showed two faults. (1) The runtime turn-sign calibration FLIPPED THE SIGN WRONG: it learns the sign by watching the derived heading rotate when turning, but in the canyon he slides along walls where movement direction follows the WALL not his facing, so the calibration read garbage; with sign=-1 the steering pressed LEFT for a right-side target, curving him away from Dollet. (2) At the start he was aimed almost dead-on at the target (err=+56) but JAMMED against the canyon wall, and the far 2400u straight-line lookahead pointed across that wall, so the wall-slide spun him ~180deg the wrong way before he broke free and committed to the wrong direction. Two fixes: the turn sign is now FIXED at +1 (TorusBearing is compass-style -- north 0, clockwise, confirmed by tgtBrg=477 for a NE target -- so pressing RIGHT turns clockwise = raises the bearing) and the wall-corrupted runtime calibration is removed; and the steering target is now DRIVE_LOOKAHEAD_CELLS(3) cells AHEAD ALONG THE ROUTE instead of the first cell >=2400u in a straight line, so the aim stays inside the winding corridor rather than cutting across a wall. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [DRIVE-DIAG] sign=+1 throughout, err to shrink as he follows the canyon, and him to head down the canyon toward Dollet rather than toward Galbadia Garden; also a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG -> false before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.62: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 ROOT-CAUSE FIX -- steer by a motion-derived heading instead of the frozen GetWorldMapHeading(). The v0.18.3.61 diagnostic proved the heading read returns a constant 540 for the ENTIRE drive (head=540 dH=+0 on every line) while position and keys varied, so every relBearing across all six prior BATs was computed against a fake facing and the steering turned the WRONG way -- it curved the player west (away from eastward Dollet) into the coastline and jammed pressing UP into collision (d+4 for ~5s). Fix: ignore GetWorldMapHeading entirely for steering and derive the facing from the bearing of the player's recent position delta (his real movement direction is exactly what steering needs), re-sampled every MOVE_HEADING_MIN_DELTA(50) units. The one unknown -- does RIGHT raise or lower that bearing -- is LEARNED at runtime: when a turn rotates the derived heading by >TURN_CAL_MIN_DELTA(80), s_driveTurnSign is set from the observed direction, so a wrong initial guess self-corrects within a sample and cannot steer wrong-way for long. Always press forward (no pivot-in-place) so motion -- and thus the heading + calibration -- never stalls; plus a wall-slide (force a turn after WALL_SLIDE_MS(600) with no movement) to nose off terrain. Replaces BOTH the mid-route and final-approach steering branches (both fed off the broken heading). [DRIVE-DIAG] now logs mh(derived) vs raw(broken) heading + signed err + learned sign. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet -- expect [DRIVE-DIAG] mh to TRACK his motion (not stuck at 540), err to shrink, and him to actually move toward Dollet; also BAT a Balamb drive (no regression). DRIVE_STEER_DIAG -> false before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.61: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 DIAGNOSTIC build (no behavior change beyond logging). Six BATs of the on-foot Dollet auto-drive have failed, each diagnosed from sparse data (a 'moved N units in 3000ms' stuck-check every 3s plus one F11), and the fixes have not converged because the actual per-tick steering behavior is not visible -- the v0.18.3.60 BAT left the player essentially stationary (16 units / 3s) near the coastal start, recovery-replanning to 6/8 before a random encounter, and the logs cannot distinguish pivot-in-place (turning, never committing forward) from forward-into-collision (UP pressed but blocked by walkmesh geometry the 1024u grid cannot see) -- which need opposite fixes. This build adds a throttled [DRIVE-DIAG] trace (DRIVE_STEER_DIAG, ~5 lines/sec) in UpdateAutoDrive logging position + delta-moved, heading + delta, target bearing, relBearing/off, keys pressed (U/L/R), and the band taken (STRAIGHT/ARC/PIVOT/final). The per-line delta-moved tells the three cases apart: PIVOT band with dH changing + d+~0 = pivoting in place; STRAIGHT/ARC with U pressed but d+~0 = walking into collision; d+ large = real progress. No steering/routing change. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet, let it sit stuck ~10s, then send ff8_world.log (the [DRIVE-DIAG] block) + an F11 -- the trace will show exactly why he is not moving, and the next build is a targeted fix. DRIVE_STEER_DIAG must be set false (or the trace removed) before the #67 push. // v0.18.3.60: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX 4 -- mid-route steering law: dead-zone + arc + clean wrap, to kill the heading-overshoot oscillation. The v0.18.3.59 BAT routed correctly (clearance-weighted 23 cells to Dollet fine(112,57)) but the drive OSCILLATED in place at the start: the player rocked across the cell-103/104 boundary (~33u E-W), creeping north only a few units, then a random encounter (MODE_SWIRL) interrupted; F11 = on foot near the coast (loco byte noise=35). Diagnosed offline against the real route -- NOT the routing or lookahead (the lookahead pointed the same way, NE ~45deg off north, from both cells he rocked between); the steering law itself. The old law walked forward (UP) only within ~17.5deg of the target and turned in place otherwise, at a fixed rate with no damping, so the heading overshot the target and rocked across it while rarely engaging forward -- weaving in place instead of driving. Across five prior BATs the routing failures (.55/.56) are fixed and the follow failures (.57 drift, .58 crawl, .59 oscillation) all trace to this one discrete turn-law. Fix: replace ONLY the mid-route branch (dist >= DRIVE_FINAL_APPROACH_DIST) with an arc law -- fold relBearing into an absolute offset + side (no hard L/R boundary to rock on), then within STEER_DEADZONE (~28deg) drive STRAIGHT with no turn (damps the overshoot -- stop correcting once roughly aimed); within STEER_FWD_CONE (~50deg) drive forward AND turn (arc); beyond it pivot in place toward the target (sharp bends, as before). Final-approach branch UNCHANGED. STEER_DEADZONE/STEER_FWD_CONE are tunable (widen deadzone if it still oscillates, narrow if it drifts into walls). This changes EVERY drive's long-distance steering, so BAT must ALSO regression-check a known-good Balamb drive. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet (thread the canyon + ARRIVE; [DRIVE] re-planning lines = recovery not failure) AND a Balamb destination (no regression). // v0.18.3.59: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX 3 -- clearance-centred routing + min-distance lookahead + stuck recovery (pre-plan the route, then drive smoothly along it). Four BATs of the fine planner each peeled a distinct bug; the last two exposed a real tension -- a far lookahead DRIFTS off the path into a wall (v0.18.3.57) while a one-cell lookahead CRAWLS (v0.18.3.58): the steering law walks forward only when the target is within ~45deg of heading and turns in place otherwise, and a 1024u target swings the bearing as the player inches, so it spins. Offline analysis showed the walkable route to Dollet is a genuinely narrow winding canyon (~1-2 fine cells wide; even a clearance-centred route averages only 1.7 cells of wall clearance), so the grid's 1024u approximation of the real walkmesh is least accurate exactly there. Three coordinated changes: (1) PlanPathFine is now a clearance-weighted Dijkstra -- s_clearFine (Chebyshev distance to the nearest blocked cell, computed once at grid-load by multi-source BFS) makes a step into a low-clearance cell cost 1 + WM_CLEAR_PENALTY*(WM_CLEAR_TARGET-clearance), so the route threads the corridor CENTRE instead of scraping walls; (2) the drive steers at the first path cell >= DRIVE_PLAN_LOOKAHEAD_DIST (2400u) ahead -- far enough for a stable bearing (no crawl), close enough that the straight-line cut stays inside the corridor (no drift); (3) mid-route stuck recovery re-plans from the player's CURRENT position (a fresh clearance route from where he actually is) up to DRIVE_MAX_REPLANS times before giving up. Container-verified on real wmx.obj: the clearance Dijkstra routes 30 cells to Dollet fine(112,57) (avg clearance 1.70, reaches the target); the offline follow-sim with the real steering law arrives with ~720u deviation at this lookahead. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet + Galbadia Garden -- expect [WALKFINE] ...clearance max/wall-hugging..., [PLAN] Fine path ...clearance-weighted, and the player to thread the canyon and ARRIVE; any [DRIVE] re-planning lines are recovery, not failure. // v0.18.3.58: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX 2 -- steer to the NEXT path cell, not a far line-of-sight lookahead. The v0.18.3.57 BAT routed correctly (27 fine cells around the cliff to Dollet) but the drive still failed ~12km out: the LOS lookahead (farthest path cell with a clear cell-centre straight line, up to 8 ahead) steered the player diagonally OFF the straight-north path corridor -- the 1024-unit cell-centre Bresenham looked clear, but the game's finer cliff geometry between cell centres blocked the diagonal, so the player drifted ~3 cells east and jammed against a cliff (stuck at fine(103,64) with the cliff cell at fine(103,63) directly north). A long lookahead cuts corners; that recurred across BATs. Fix: the drive now steers to s_drivePath[idx+1] only -- consecutive path cells are 4-adjacent and walkable, so the player can't cut a corner or drift off-corridor, and the cursor's per-tick re-projection to the nearest path cell steers him back on if a collision nudges him off. Trades steering smoothness for faithfully hugging the route (FineLineWalkable now unused). BAT: auto-drive to Dollet + Galbadia Garden -- the player should follow the path corridor around the cliff and ARRIVE. // v0.18.3.57: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 FIX -- route to the destination coordinate, not the goal region. The v0.18.3.56 BAT failed to reach Dollet: PlanPathFine routed to the nearest cell of the goal REGION, but Dollet's region 0x01 is large and reaches right next to the player, so the fine path was only 3 cells north and the drive then steered NE into the same cliff. Fix: PlanPathFine now floods the reachable component from the player and routes to the visited fine cell NEAREST the destination coordinate (s_driveTargetX/Y) -- the target itself when reachable, which the catalog already vetted. s_driveGoalSegs is retained for arrival detection only. Container-verified on real wmx.obj: Dollet now routes 20 fine cells AROUND the cliff to the actual target fine(112,57) (dist 0), zero blocked cells on the path. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet + Galbadia Garden -- expect ff8_world.log '[PLAN] Fine path: N cells ... nearest reachable to target' and arrival, not a hang. // v0.18.3.56: #67 BAT 2 stage 2 -- point the auto-drive planner at the fine slope-aware grid. The coarse 32x24 A* (PlanPath over s_terrainGrid, segment-centre waypoints) routed straight lines through mountains it couldn't see -- the v0.18.3.55 BAT hung the Dollet drive at the base of a steep cliff because the planner laid a waypoint due north into terrain the fine grid already knew was impassable. PlanPathFine replaces it: a 4-connected torus BFS over the 256x192 fine grid using the SAME IsFineTraversable rule as catalog reachability (s_walkClassFine + s_steepFine), to the nearest walkable fine cell in the goal region (s_driveGoalSegs), storing the route as packed fine cells in s_drivePath. The drive follows it with a line-of-sight lookahead (FineLineWalkable Bresenham, up to 8 cells): it steers toward the farthest path cell reachable in a clear walkable straight line, smoothing diagonal staircases AND guaranteeing it never aims the player across a blocked cell (a fixed lookahead would cut the cliff corner). On final approach / path end it steers to the real destination coord. Region/goal selection (MatchProgramForCatalog/CollectGoalSegments) is unchanged -- only the routing grid changed; geometric-trigger destinations (Fire Cavern, early Balamb Garden) still use simple-coord steering and are unaffected. Container-verified on real wmx.obj: the Dollet route is 20 fine cells AROUND the cliff (fine(101,65)->fine(112,57), zero blocked cells); LOS lookahead crosses no blocked cell at any cursor. BAT: auto-drive to Dollet and to Galbadia Garden -- ff8_world.log should show '[PLAN] Fine path: N cells', and the drive should route around mountains and ARRIVE instead of hanging. // v0.18.3.55: #67 BAT 2 stage 1 -- slope-aware fine grid (mountains). The fine reachability grid now carries a per-cell steepness (the elevation spread of the wmx polygon at the cell, read from the vertex elevation field previously discarded during the wmx load). IsFineTraversable blocks a MOUNTAIN-class cell whose steepness exceeds WM_MTN_STEEP_BLOCK (256) for foot/chocobo/car -- steep mountain faces are impassable; gentle mountain cells (passes/plateaus) stay walkable, so the map doesn't over-fragment (blanket type-29 blocking isolated Esthar and split Edea from Centra -- real passes exist). Garden/Ragnarok ignore it (hover/fly). Threshold calibrated offline against destination reachability: at 256, every Galbadia and Balamb catalog destination stays reachable AND the full reachable set is unchanged vs no blocking; it is refined in-game via the [WM-CALIB] steepness trace (now logs steep=N plus a BLOCKED flag per cell). Empirical terrain re-eval also confirmed the six undocumented terrain types present (17/18/23/24/25/31) all sit at land elevations and are gentle -- genuine walkable land, NOT hidden water; all map water is the ocean class already blocked. This stage touches reachability/catalog only; the AD planner still routes on the coarse grid (stage 2 points it at the fine grid). BAT: load a Galbadia save then a Balamb save and cycle the catalog -- expect the SAME destinations as v0.18.3.54 (none lost); check ff8_world.log [WALKFINE] (mountain steep-blocked count) and [WM-CALIB] (it should never log the player standing on a BLOCKED cell -- if it does, the threshold is too low).
